     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Black hole jets reach far beyond galaxies' visible edges, potentially deciding their fate</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#astronomy</span> <span>#galaxies</span> <span>#black holes</span> <span>#CGM</span></div>
       <p>Galaxies are vast structures containing hundreds of billions of stars, which derive their formation from cold, dense gas. Every large galaxy, including our own Milky Way, is surrounded by a massive envelope of gas known as the circumgalactic medium, or CGM.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/black-hole-jets-reach-far-beyond-galaxies-visible-edges-pote.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This reservoir serves as a primary source of raw material for star formation. Research indicates that the CGM extends significantly, reaching 10 to 20 times the diameter of the galaxy's visible portion. </p><p>Recent scientific observation focuses on how black hole jets interact with this expansive gas environment. Because the CGM is so much larger than the visible galaxy, the reach of these jets can extend far beyond the visible boundaries, potentially influencing the availability of the gas needed to form new stars. </p><p>Understanding the dynamics of this gas reservoir is essential to determining the long-term evolution and ultimate fate of galaxies. The interaction between internal black hole activity and this external gas cloud remains a key area of study in galactic development.</p><p>What to watch: Further data on how black hole jets physically alter the density of the circumgalactic medium.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the source material while maintaining an appropriate journalistic tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-black-hole-jets-galaxies-visible.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
