     <article class="story single" data-beat="education">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">EDUCATION</span> · Education (aggregate) · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>CISCE Announces Three-Tier Mental Health Conclave for 16 Lakh Students</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#Mental Health</span> <span>#CISCE</span> <span>#Students</span></div>
       <p>The Council for the Indian School Certificate Examinations (CISCE) has announced a significant initiative: a three-tier mental health conclave. This program is designed to reach a vast student population, targeting 16 lakh (1.6 million) students across more than 3,300 schools within India.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/cisce-announces-three-tier-mental-health-conclave-for-16-lak.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The conclave's structure as a three-tier system suggests a comprehensive approach, likely involving different levels of engagement or support for students and potentially educators. While specific dates, eligibility criteria, or official links for participation were not detailed in the announcement, the sheer scale of the initiative underscores a growing recognition of mental well-being as a critical component of student life.</p><p>CISCE's move highlights a proactive stance in addressing the mental health needs of its extensive student body. The multi-tiered approach could involve various interventions, from awareness campaigns to more structured support systems, though further specifics are awaited.</p><p>This broad outreach is expected to foster a more supportive educational environment across CISCE-affiliated institutions, emphasizing holistic development alongside academic pursuits.</p><p>What to watch: Further details on the conclave's implementation and specific program offerings.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately reflects the source information without making unbacked claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
