     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>DNA Reveals Chemosynthesis Genes in Bacteria on Coral Reef Fish Gills</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Biology</span> <span>#Marine Life</span> <span>#Microbiology</span></div>
       <p>A new discovery has identified chemosynthetic bacteria residing on the gills of coral reef fish, marking a previously undocumented association within the marine ecosystem. While these bacteria are known to exist in various marine environments and with invertebrate hosts like annelid worms and mollusks, their presence in fish represents a novel finding.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/dna-reveals-chemosynthesis-genes-in-bacteria-on-coral-reef-f.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The research indicates that fish gills harbor a complex and specialized microbiome. This microbial community is currently not fully understood, but its potential role in the host fish's metabolism, immunity, and overall health is significant. The discovery opens new avenues for understanding the intricate relationships between fish and their microbial inhabitants.</p><p>Chemosynthesis, a process where organisms derive energy from chemical reactions rather than sunlight, is a fundamental biological pathway in many extreme environments. Identifying bacteria capable of this process on fish gills suggests a unique symbiotic relationship that could contribute to the fish's physiological functions. Further investigation is needed to fully elucidate the mechanisms and benefits of this interaction. The specialized microbiome on fish gills could offer crucial insights into aquatic health and ecosystem dynamics.</p><p>What to watch: Further studies exploring the specific functions and implications of these chemosynthetic bacteria for fish health.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the provided summary without introducing unverified claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-dna-reveals-chemosynthesis-genes-bacteria.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-09-27/dna-reveals-chemosynthesis-genes-in-bacteria-on-coral-reef-f" data-title="DNA Reveals Chemosynthesis Genes in Bacteria on Coral Reef Fish Gills"><b>Share:</b>
