     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · News18 India · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Name Missing From SIR? If Tribunals Take A Decade To Decide Appeals, What Happens To Your Right To Vote</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Indian Constitution</span> <span>#Right to Vote</span> <span>#Judicial Delays</span> <span>#Democracy</span></div>
       <p>Delays in the judicial process, specifically concerning the resolution of appeals related to the Special Identification Register (SIR), are raising significant constitutional concerns. Advocate Jatin Bhardwaj has highlighted that if tribunals take an extensive period, potentially a decade, to decide these appeals, it could have profound implications for a citizen's fundamental right to vote.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/name-missing-from-sir-if-tribunals-take-a-decade-to-decide-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Such prolonged delays directly impinge upon a voter’s constitutional entitlements, as articulated under Articles 14 and 21 of the Indian Constitution. Article 14 ensures equality before the law and equal protection of the laws, while Article 21 guarantees the protection of life and personal liberty. The right to vote, a cornerstone of democratic participation, is intrinsically linked to these fundamental rights.</p><p>Bhardwaj's observations underscore a critical issue within the legal framework, suggesting that the efficiency of the appellate process is vital for safeguarding democratic principles. When an individual’s name is missing from the SIR and their appeal for inclusion faces a decade-long wait, their ability to exercise their franchise is effectively suspended for an unacceptably long duration. This situation necessitates a closer examination of judicial timelines and their impact on civic rights.</p><p>What to watch: The debate surrounding judicial delays and their impact on fundamental voting rights.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source material and correctly attributes the legal concerns to advocate Jatin Bhardwaj.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/india/name-missing-from-voter-roll-after-sir-if-tribunals-take-a-decade-to-decide-appeals-what-happens-to-your-right-to-vote-supreme-court-election-commission-10355207.html" rel="noopener">News18 India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
