     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · TOI Entertainment · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Sanjay slams smoking posters: Filmmaker questions macho ‘smoker’ heroes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Sanjay Gupta</span> <span>#Smoking Posters</span> <span>#CBFC</span> <span>#Bollywood</span></div>
       <p>Filmmaker Sanjay Gupta has ignited a significant online debate by publicly questioning the prevalent imagery of smoking heroes on recent Bollywood movie posters. Gupta specifically called out promotional materials for films such as 'Animal', 'Dhurandhar', and 'Love &amp; War', highlighting a concern about the glorification of smoking.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/sanjay-slams-smoking-posters-filmmaker-questions-macho-smoke.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>His critique comes at a time when the Central Board of Film Certification (CBFC) has introduced stricter regulations concerning the depiction of narcotics in films. These new rules, which mandate anti-drug warnings, were officially notified on September 16, indicating a broader push for responsible content portrayal within the industry.</p><p>Further emphasizing the regulatory shift, the Centre recently reconstituted the CBFC board. An 18-member panel has been established, featuring prominent personalities including actress Preity Zinta and actor Pankaj Tripathi, among others. This reconstitution suggests a renewed focus on censorship and content guidelines. Gupta's intervention adds a voice from within the filmmaking community to this ongoing discussion about cinematic responsibility and public health messaging.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of new CBFC rules and filmmaker dialogues on future movie poster designs.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary without adding unverified information.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/hindi/bollywood/news/what-is-the-point-of-unpleasant-anti-smoking-reels-sanjay-gupta-slams-smoking-posters-of-animal-dhurandhar-love-war/articleshow/134520964.cms" rel="noopener">TOI Entertainment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
