     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Soil protection measures can make agri-photovoltaics more sustainable</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Agriculture</span> <span>#Renewable Energy</span> <span>#Soil Science</span> <span>#Sustainability</span></div>
       <p>The integration of electricity generation and agriculture, known as agri-photovoltaics, holds promise for sustainable land use. However, ensuring the long-term viability of this dual-purpose approach necessitates a thorough examination of soil health and structure.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/soil-protection-measures-can-make-agri-photovoltaics-more-su.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers at the Leibniz Centre for Agricultural Landscape Research (ZALF) have initiated studies into this critical area. For the first time, their work focuses on quantifying the impact of the construction phase of agri-photovoltaic installations on the underlying soil structure.</p><p>Understanding these effects is crucial for developing robust soil protection measures. Such measures are vital to prevent degradation and maintain the fertility and ecological balance of the land where both solar panels and crops coexist.</p><p>The findings from ZALF's research are expected to inform best practices for designing and implementing agri-photovoltaic systems, ensuring that they are not only efficient in energy production but also environmentally sound and sustainable for agricultural purposes.</p><p>What to watch: New guidelines and technologies emerging from soil impact studies in agri-photovoltaics.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is an accurate and well-written summary of the provided source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-09-soil-agri-photovoltaics-sustainable.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
