     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tamil Nadu Government Reverses RTI Exemption Amid Public Outcry</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tamil Nadu</span> <span>#RTI</span> <span>#Law and Order</span> <span>#Transparency</span></div>
       <p>Chennai, India – The Tamil Nadu government has reversed a recent decision to exempt its Public (Law and Order) Department from the Right to Information (RTI) Act, following widespread criticism. The initial notification had drawn significant backlash from various segments of society, particularly from numerous political leaders across the state.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/tamil-nadu-government-reverses-rti-exemption-amid-public-out.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Critics had vocally argued that such an exemption would fundamentally undermine governmental transparency and obstruct the public's access to vital records pertaining to law enforcement and public order. Concerns were raised about the potential for reduced accountability and openness within a key administrative department. The consensus among opponents was that the move jeopardized the democratic principle of citizens' right to information.</p><p>Responding to the considerable public and political pressure, the state government acted swiftly to withdraw the controversial exemption. In doing so, it reiterated its commitment to the foundational principles of the RTI Act, which is designed to ensure transparency and accountability in governance. The reversal ensures that the Public (Law and Order) Department remains subject to public scrutiny under the Act.</p><p>This development highlights the significant influence of public opinion and political advocacy in shaping policy decisions related to governmental transparency in the state. The government's quick turnaround suggests a responsiveness to concerns regarding democratic rights.</p><p>What to watch: The long-term implications for transparency and public access to information in state governance.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately summarizes the provided source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/tamil-nadu-govt-rolls-back-law-and-order-departments-rti-exemption-amid-backlash/articleshow/134519738.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
