     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tribal students to launch 180-km march to Mumbai on September 28 over poor residential school facilities</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#National News</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Maharashtra</span> <span>#Tribal Rights</span></div>
       <p>A significant protest is set to unfold in Maharashtra as tribal students prepare to embark on a 180-kilometre march to Mumbai. The demonstration, scheduled to commence on September 28, is organized to highlight and demand action regarding the persistently poor facilities in tribal schools and hostels across the state. This long-distance march underscores the gravity of the issues faced by these students.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/tribal-students-to-launch-180-km-march-to-mumbai-on-septembe.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The students' movement is driven by a set of clear and pressing demands aimed at improving their living and learning conditions. Key among these demands is the immediate resignation of Maharashtra Tribal Development Minister Ashok Uike. Additionally, the protesters are calling for the transfer of both the Tribal Development Department’s Secretary and Commissioner, indicating a call for accountability and systemic change within the administration responsible for their welfare.</p><p>Furthermore, a central focus of the march is the urgent need for basic facilities in tribal schools and hostels. This encompasses essential amenities that are often lacking, impacting the students' health, safety, and educational environment. The march to the state capital is intended to draw widespread attention to these critical issues and pressure authorities to implement lasting solutions.</p><p>What to watch: The progress of the march and the government's response to the students' demands.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately conveys the key demands and the nature of the protest mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/maharashtra/tribal-students-to-launch-180-km-march-to-mumbai-on-september-28-over-poor-residential-school-facilities/article71515728.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/mumbai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mumbai</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-09-27/tribal-students-to-launch-180-km-march-to-mumbai-on-septembe" data-title="Tribal students to launch 180-km march to Mumbai on September 28 over poor residential school facilities"><b>Share:</b>
         <a class="sh-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg class="ic" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M8 1.8A6.2 6.2 0 0 0 2.7 11L1.8 14.2l3.3-.9A6.2 6.2 0 1 0 8 1.8z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>WhatsApp</a> ·
