     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-09-27 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>US States Address Rising Bear-Human Encounters</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Wildlife</span> <span>#USA</span> <span>#Conservation</span></div>
       <p>Across the United States, bears are increasingly venturing into human inhabited areas, leading to a rise in encounters. This trend is presenting significant challenges for wildlife management agencies, compelling their personnel to make difficult decisions regarding the animals and public safety.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-27/us-states-address-rising-bear-human-encounters.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Dan Thompson, who serves as the supervisor of the large carnivore section at the Wyoming Game and Fish Department, finds satisfaction in his field work. His responsibilities include capturing various large carnivores, such as mountain lions, wolves, black bears, and grizzly bears, primarily for study purposes. Thompson expressed his long-standing fascination with these animals.</p><p>&quot;I have always been personally intrigued with carnivores and their role throughout any ecosystem,&quot; Thompson stated. He further added, &quot;I actually feel very fortunate to get paid to do what we do.&quot; His comments highlight the dedication of wildlife workers who manage these complex interactions. The increasing presence of bears in residential zones underscores a shifting dynamic between wildlife and human populations.</p><p>Wildlife officials are tasked with balancing the conservation of these species with the safety of communities. The situation requires careful consideration and strategic approaches to mitigate potential conflicts and ensure the well-being of both humans and wildlife.</p><p>What to watch: Efforts by wildlife departments to manage bear populations and public safety in affected areas.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article faithfully captures the tone and content of the source summary and quoted material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/27/bear-human-encounters" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
